sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: stage 4 of the command development pipeline (docs/reference/pipeline/4-pr-and-ci.mmd, "Admit a review round"): one Swamp run per round. The pattern of task:prep-fix: one admission, one parent Swamp run, existing workflows as children. It replaces the gate check driving the reviewer turn, the comment and the fix one admission at a time (about 60 s per hop). The comment steps are steps of this run; the standalone `tasks.pr_comment` operation and its chart are gone. Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review.
  %% status: draft
  %% kind: tasks.review_round
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane VM icon=terminal pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane GH icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant VM as Task VM<br/>reviewer · author
  participant GH as GitHub<br/>PR
  Note over GW: Admit the round<br/>group · head · round N
  %%> Who calls it:
  %%> - the gate check `task:pr-gate`, once for each head of a group in automatic mode; there is no command, route or MCP tool
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - the group is an open external group of a review-and-merge Project, with a published PR that is out of draft, and no running operation
  %%> - the PR head is eligible: a passed handoff candidate of the current subtask, or the `pushed_commit` of an earlier round of this group; a pushed fix has no hosted handoff CI, because the PR's own CI is the merge check below, and the row of its local check is no candidate for a review turn or a manual publish, because no `tasks.check` operation backs it
  %%> - the group used fewer than 3 rounds, and the merge check of the PR is green on the head
  %%> - the task VM of the group is parked or running, and its Pi credential is there
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - captures, in one transaction, the resume of the VM and the reviewer turn in a new thread, with the repository and PR number of the comment
  %%> - in rounds 1 and 2 it also captures the fix: the author turn, the local check and the push. Round 3 captures no fix, so findings in round 3 end the run
  %%> - creates the check row of the fix as `running` and puts its id in the intent; the check has no hosted CI, so it needs no CI token
  %%> - the row belongs to the current open subtask of the group, as `task:check` does today; the base, the deliverables and the empty setup come from that subtask
  %%> - names the vault keys of the Pi token, the comment token and the push token; the run writes the Pi token when it starts, and each GitHub step mints its token when it runs
  %%> - records the round (group, head, round number, operation, check row) in the same transaction; the round row has the new columns `pushed_commit` and `check_id`, and the three `fix_*_operation_id` columns are gone
  %%> - a repeat tick on the same head replays this Operation; the gate check admits nothing else for a round: no comment, no fix turn, no check and no push
  %%> Not captured:
  %%> - the findings: the fix prompt, the comment body and the pushed commit are built by the run from the settled review
  GW->>SW: Start one run, full intent<br/>operation-task-review-round
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209); no Node is a target
  %%> Inputs:
  %%> - the whole intent above and the vault key names of the credentials, never their values
  %%> - two steps of the run call the Gateway: the comment and the push mint their tokens when they start, with the run id of this run
  SW->>VM: Resume the VM<br/>operation-task-vm-resume
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - starts the parked VM and waits for its agent and Pi; the workspace and the Pi sessions are as the park left them
  SW->>VM: Review the head<br/>operation-task-turn
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - a new reviewer thread, with the role `reviewer`: an agent that took no part in the spec, the code or an earlier round
  %%> - it reads the PR head, the chart, the page, the cases and the CI result, and judges them against the review rules; it changes no file
  VM-->>SW: Pass<br/>or findings
  Note over SW: Settle the review<br/>verdict · findings
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the new `round` method of `@nckrtl/task-turn-data` turns the outcome of the reviewer into a verdict: pass, findings, or failed (a turn that left no result is failed)
  %%> - the findings become the end of the prompt of the fix turn and the body of the comment
  SW->>GW: Mint the comment token<br/>operation:direct:github-token
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - mints a token with only `pull_requests: write` into the vault key that admission named
  SW->>GH: Check the PR head
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the step `Read the PR` reads the state and head of the PR, and this step refuses a PR that is not open or whose head is not the head of the round, with `tasks_pr.head_moved`; both may fail without failing the run, because the push refuses a moved branch itself
  SW->>GH: Post the comment
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - posts the verdict and the findings as a comment of the App, so Nick can read them
  %%> - the step may fail without failing the run: a comment that GitHub refused never loses the verdict or the fix
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the App opened the PR, and GitHub does not let an author approve its own PR, so the pass is recorded by the Gateway, not as a GitHub approval
  SW->>GH: Clean up the token<br/>task-github-cleanup
  %%> When:
  %%> - always, on success and failure
  opt the review has findings and the round is below 3
    SW->>VM: Fix the findings<br/>operation-task-turn
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - the author thread of the group gets the findings as its prompt and fixes them in the workspace
    %%> Refuses:
    %%> - a turn that does not end with the outcome `ready_for_review`, read by the `outcome` method of `@nckrtl/task-turn-data` and checked by the step `Check the fix turn` (`tasks.review_fix_not_ready`): nothing is checked or pushed
    SW->>VM: Check the fix<br/>operation-task-check
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - the handoff check of the Project on the workspace, as `task:check` runs it, without hosted CI (`check_name` is null): the PR runs its own CI on the pushed head
    Note over SW: Settle the check<br/>commit · tree
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - the new `checked` method of `@nckrtl/task-turn-data` reads the output of the check: passed, and the commit and tree to push
    %%> Refuses:
    %%> - a check that did not pass, or that ends `changed`, because the author must make the commit; the step `Check the fix passed` fails with `tasks.review_fix_not_passed`: nothing is pushed
    SW->>GW: Mint the push token<br/>operation:direct:github-token
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - the child `operation-task-publish` mints the token when it starts, with the run id of this run
    SW->>GH: Push the fix<br/>operation-task-publish
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - pushes the checked commit to the branch of the PR without force, with `check: null` as `task:prep-fix` does; the commit, tree and refspec come from the output of the check
    %%> - the head moves; once the merge check is green on it, the gate check admits the next round
    %%> Refuses:
    %%> - a branch that moved since the park, with `tasks.not_fast_forward`; nothing is overwritten
  end
  Note over SW: Confirm the round<br/>verdict · push
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the last step ends the run in error, with `tasks.review_round_failed`, when the reviewer left no verdict, or when a round that captured a fix has findings and the push did not succeed
  %%> - round 3 captured no fix, so findings there end the run well, and the gate check sends the group back to manual mode
  alt the run succeeded
    SW-->>GW: Native terminal result<br/>verdict · commit
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway
    Note over GW: Record the round<br/>verdict · check row
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the Gateway records the verdict and the findings of that head and round, updates the check row from the output of the check, and records the pushed commit on the round, so that the next admission accepts it as a head
    %%> - a pass leaves no fix: the check row is set to `cancelled`; round 3 captured none
    %%> - a pass lets the gate check admit the merge step
    %%> - findings with a pushed commit leave the group in automatic mode: the head moves, and the gate check admits the next round on it
  else the run failed, was cancelled or was interrupted
    SW--xGW: Native failed result<br/>error_code · failed step · verdict
    %%> The Gateway reads:
    %%> - which step failed and its error code; the result keeps `findings` when the review ended with findings, even if the fix did not pass, and never keeps a `pass`: a run that ended in error is `failed`, so the merge step cannot follow it
    Note over GW: Record the round<br/>failed · fall back
    %%> Result:
    %%> - every end records the round: the verdict the run reached, or `failed` when it reached none, so the gate never waits on a round that ended; the check row is closed as `failed` or `cancelled`
    %%> - a budget expiry or a start that Swamp refused ends the round as `failed` the same way, with no terminal event of the run
    %%> - after round 3 with findings, a failed round, or a failed fix, the gate check switches the group to manual mode; the ready step then requests the review of Nick
    %%> Not built:
    %%> - the same switch when the merge check is red on a head that a round pushed: the gate check keeps skipping that head, as it did before this run existed
    %%> - a group that is cancelled or completed stops the run as `task:cancel` stops any operation of the group, and nothing falls back
  end
